  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1254 AM MDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nAn upper level high will be centered over the southwest part of \nthe country through Sunday. This will result in west-northwest \nflow aloft across north central and northeast Colorado. This \npattern will keep wildfire smoke in the area for the foreseeable \nfuture. For today, cross sections show a very dry airmass with \nclear skies in place through the morning. High-based cumulus \nclouds will develop this afternoon and then drift eastward. \nTemperatures at 700mb are very close to yesterday's, so expect \nhigh temperatures similar to yesterday with mid to upper 90s \nacross the urban corridor\u00a0and northeast plains. Airmass warms a \nlittle Saturday, thus high temperatures will be warmer, \nranging\u00a0from 97 to 105 across the urban corridor and northeast \nplains. Winds will pick up over north central Colorado during the \nafternoon, with gusts around 25 mph, which will elevate the fire \ndanger. Some moisture is able to infiltrate the upper level high \noverhead Sunday, pushing precipitable water values\u00a0close to normal\nover the higher terrain. This is expected to lead to isolated to \nscattered afternoon thunderstorms. The storms will have difficulty\nsurviving off the higher terrain, so will keep the mention of \nshowers/storms west of I-25. Temperatures take a small step back \nSunday due to the increase in moisture and clouds.\n\nVery warm temperatures are expected to continue Monday and \nTuesday. The ridge will weaken slightly and drift south a little. \nTemperatures won't be as hot but highs are still expected to be in\nthe mid to upper 90s across northeast Colorado. Moisture will \ncontinue to slowly increase with isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms expected each day. Best moisture will be over the \nhigher terrain and the bulk of the showers/storms will be found \nthere. Not much change from the GFS and ECMWF over the past 24 \nhours. The GFS shows the ridge remaining in place with warmer \ntemperatures and limited monsoonal moisture. The ECMWF weakens the\nhigh and shows it sliding off to the southeast, which would bring\nbetter monsoonal moisture beginning Wednesday.",
